    "media:imagetext": "ladl\nCONFIDENTAL\nМит ав.\naN u\nHongkong - June 10th '49\nDear Family:\nI left San Francisco May 24th on one of PAA's new Boeing Streto-\ncruisers which carries 61 passengers on the upper deck and has space for 17.\nmore in a lower deck lounge reached by a eireuler staircase. The plane 1s\npressurized right from sea-level so there is no effect on your eers at all.\nOur crossing in ten hours compares with my first crossing 13 years ago of 20\nhours or more.\nBondy met me in Honolulu and drove me to the Royal Hawelian where \u0026\nroom had been reserved for me.\nThe guests all looked 11ke black market op-\nerators and I guess to stay there they have to be. I asked for a special rate\nand got it - otherwise would have paid 130 per day.\nThe second day I \"phoned\nDebby Blekewell over on Kaual and asked her to have lunch with me at the most\nattractive Kausi Inn near Lahul.\nShe wes so exeited that she nearly dropped\nthe iphone and I had to repeat everything I said to be sure she got it all\nstraight.\nKaual is only an hour by sir from Honolulu but the airport is at\none end of the Island and Debby and Pompy at the other end some 40 minutes\ndrive by car. Debby met me in her British Ford - Pompy joined us for Lunch\nand we had lots of walla welle, sew the kids - very cute - the new house on\nthe beach which is just perfect, and then they drove me back to the airport.\nLeft Honolulu the next afternoon in a DC-l, Bleeperette and flew direct\nto fake arriving there in the middle of the night so couldn't work the beach\nfor sea shells as I had on my previous trip. On to Tokyo arriving there May\n30 early in the afternoon.\nHe laid over there until 2:30 a.m. when we de-\nparted for Okinava - e refueling stop while Shanghei is closed .\nWe arrived\nthere esrly in the morning and atayed on the ground about an hour and one-half.\nThe harbors are dotted with the wrecks of ships many of which were American\nboats caught there in a typhoon after V-J Day.\nThere was a long stretch of besch\nnesr the airport but the PAA manager seid no shells.\nThe latter are on the\nPacific side some ten milea awey where the boya go swimming. He is going to\nget some for me including \"cats' eyes\" which normally are found only in New\nZealand.\nWe arrived Hongkong after a flight along the east coast of Formosa.\nThe mountains of that island\nrise up 1400 feet and while our direct flight\nfrom Okinews to Hongkong would take us directly across Formosa we don't fly\nthat high in the DC-4's so - as previously stated - flew'along a nost for\nbiddire coast. The mountains come right down to the beach or terminate in\nabrupt cliffs against which break the long rollers of the Pacifie. In more\nthan 30 miles of coast line I saw no sign of habitation either on the coast\nor beek in the mountains - where dwell, I am told, an unconquered aborigine\nmountain people who have held those mountain fastneases against Chinese and\nJapanese alike.\nToward the southern tip of Formosa the mountains trail off\ninto a long flat point where there are small villages and farms. We flew over\nusT lalled To d. l Man\nAESISIHOS",
